# Trivy vulnerability exceptions — one CVE ID per line, with a comment
# explaining why it's safe to ignore. Prefer fixing over adding entries
# here; only add when there's a genuine, documented reason a real finding
# doesn't apply.

# --- apps/api image: Debian OS-level dependencies with no upstream fix.
# Two distinct sources, both genuinely unavoidable — verified, not assumed:
#   1. Google Chrome's own .deb package (`playwright install chrome` — see
#      apps/api/Dockerfile) installs with its full Recommends: chain (fonts,
#      perl, glib, cups, ncurses, curl, openssl, libssh2...) — switching from
#      the generic Chromium build to the "chrome" channel the FFF scraper
#      requires (fff-scraper.service.ts uses `channel: 'chrome'`) didn't
#      reduce this, it's inherent to installing Chrome's actual package.
#   2. Packages already present in the bare `node:22-bookworm-slim` base
#      image BEFORE anything in this Dockerfile runs (e.g. util-linux) —
#      confirmed with `docker run --rm node:22-bookworm-slim dpkg -l`.
#      Nothing to opt out of; it ships with the base image itself.
# `apt-get upgrade` (already run in the Dockerfile) has nothing newer for
# any of these — Trivy marks them "affected"/"will_not_fix"/"fix_deferred",
# no Debian bookworm fix published (re-verified via `apt list --upgradable`
# inside the built image — empty). None of it is reachable application
# attack surface: the scraper only ever visits one fixed, trusted FFF domain
# over HTTPS, in pure headless mode (no X server, no user-supplied files,
# no LDAP/SSH/TIFF/mount processing) — this whole dependency surface sits
# unused by our actual application code.
CVE-2022-4055
CVE-2023-25193
CVE-2023-2953
CVE-2023-45853
CVE-2023-52355
CVE-2025-69720
CVE-2025-7458
